<pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I656b432e3272646ad68f8d793117c0bb32578a36" commit="8918f57c65780801b350b3148c108ba18fa2b0bb" commit_message="chromeos-config: Add the platform name property to identity  This is needed to distinguish different SKU maps within the same family. Add this property to the schema.  Also make the sku-id optional, since not all models have it.  These changes bring identity into line with the current master config format.  BUG=chromium:796983 TEST=see that the schema validation for reef works (it includes this field)  Change-Id: I656b432e3272646ad68f8d793117c0bb32578a36 Signed-off-by: Simon Glass &lt;sjg@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/919243 Reviewed-by: C Shapiro &lt;shapiroc@google.com&gt; " fail_count="2" gerrit_number="919243" owner_email="sjg@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/43/919243/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="2"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I31429aac08db736c07d05cd465279456fa3c957f" commit="c14b7244bddb526162aa8fd1a901c721e2ad6c21" commit_message="chromeos-config: Support outputting a list of phandle properties  A long-standing niggle with the device-tree config is that the phandle property names are hard-coded, rather than being read from the schema. Add a command that works similarly to the target directories feature, adding the information to the config. This can then be read by cros_config at run time.  BUG=chromium:796983 TEST=sudo emerge -q --nodeps chromeos-config-tools chromeos-config-host emerge-reef chromeos-firmware-reef fdtdump ~/cosarm/chroot/build/reef/usr/share/chromeos-config/config.dtb See that the schema info is present:    phandle-properties = &quot;arc-properties-type&quot;, &quot;audio-type&quot;, &quot;bcs-type&quot;,       &quot;power-type&quot;, &quot;shares&quot;, &quot;single-sku&quot;, &quot;touch-type&quot;, &quot;whitelabel&quot;;  Change-Id: I31429aac08db736c07d05cd465279456fa3c957f Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/919244 Commit-Ready: Simon Glass &lt;sjg@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Simon Glass &lt;sjg@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: C Shapiro &lt;shapiroc@google.com&gt; " fail_count="2" gerrit_number="919244" owner_email="sjg@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/44/919244/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="2"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-4.4" change_id="I24c0c2a6a4285a8d33833c7063649ddcd421a2f2" commit="d2d9bfa5cafca9f74e5e97861d3d3e0d4831fc64" commit_message="CHROMIUM: pkglist: Add dependency on CONFIG_FS  It is currently possible to build PKGLIST into the kernel while CONFIGFS_FS is built as module. Doing so results in builds failures due to missing symbols. To avoid the problem, restrict PKGLIST to be buildable only as module if CONFIGFS_FS is built as module.  BUG=b:63876697 TEST=Try to build with and without CONFIG_FS enabled  Change-Id: I24c0c2a6a4285a8d33833c7063649ddcd421a2f2 Signed-off-by: Guenter Roeck &lt;groeck@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/919541 Reviewed-by: Sarthak Kukreti &lt;sarthakkukreti@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="3" gerrit_number="919541" owner_email="groeck@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/41/919541/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-4.4" change_id="If1d833578c1f2c1f25462d72c6de725147a41563" commit="a5c0b5df0cea7937e89c0257563a1507d7a406f2" commit_message="CHROMIUM: security: export security_path_chown  esdfs calls security_path_chown, which is not exported. Attempts to build it as module fail with  ERROR: &quot;security_path_chown&quot; [fs/esdfs/esdfs.ko] undefined!  Making it boolean would also require making PKGLIST, which it depends on, boolean. Let's export security_path_chown instead.  BUG=b:63876697 TEST=compilation, mount esdfs  Change-Id: If1d833578c1f2c1f25462d72c6de725147a41563 Signed-off-by: Guenter Roeck &lt;groeck@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/919542 Reviewed-by: Sarthak Kukreti &lt;sarthakkukreti@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="3" gerrit_number="919542" owner_email="groeck@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/42/919542/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I5f1a2f7b57b189c62890303c3c2f4afab752166f" commit="efeb9b75d8d650de6e3de3545d3c55cf0906f34d" commit_message="init: Preserve tpm_firmware_update_request in clobber-state  This file indicates that a TPM firmware update has been requested and triggers update installation after TPM clear. When installing a TPM firmware update as part of powerwash, we need to preserve the file such that the update installation actually triggers after powerwash.  BUG=chromium:768342 TEST=Manual  Change-Id: I5f1a2f7b57b189c62890303c3c2f4afab752166f Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/848774 Commit-Ready: Mattias Nissler &lt;mnissler@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Mattias Nissler &lt;mnissler@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Thiemo Nagel &lt;tnagel@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Andrey Pronin &lt;apronin@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="848774" owner_email="mnissler@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="8" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/74/848774/8" remote="cros" total_fail_count="2"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Icc0da552491ce086b7bef7d9926d94ee14a048ac" commit="ca2acb748dd4caa37f174e19edceebf461a303c4" commit_message="Add chromeos-ec-private-files  In order to guarantee that the chromeos-base/chromeos-ec builds will also contain the private sources (from chromeos/platform/ec-private repository) if available (and still do not fail the build if not present), as explained in the linked issue, we introduce a chromeos-ec-private-files virtual ebuild which points either to chromeos-ec-private-files-null (no private source, empty private sub-directory) or the private chromeos-base/chromeos-ec-private-files which copies the private repository into ${SYSROOT}/firmware/ec-private to have them available for the main chromeos-ec package build.  BUG=chromium:804298 TEST=run a tryjob for meowth-release and see that the meowth_fp ec binary contains the private bits, run the same tryjob without the private CL and see it succeed. TEST=run locally: 'cros_workon-meowth start chromeos-base/chromeos-ec-private-files' and 'emerge-meowth chromeos-base/chromeos-ec-private-files'  Change-Id: Icc0da552491ce086b7bef7d9926d94ee14a048ac Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/891185 Commit-Ready: Vincent Palatin &lt;vpalatin@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Vincent Palatin &lt;vpalatin@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Randall Spangler &lt;rspangler@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="891185" owner_email="vpalatin@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/85/891185/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ia8416f0af25cf929d5684b9252c002626b526100" commit="996052c9aa5a9329a643e3315d1813dff7b46611" commit_message="[Autotest]files_CopyFileToGoogleDriveUI: Add test to copy file to Google drive  Script performs the following steps 1. Open Files application 2. Create a file in local storage 3. Copy the file from local storage to Google drive 4. Validate whether the file is